1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
This is Task 5, feature development with branching.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:14,000
A feature branch is a separate line of development for one feature, so unfinished work never disturbs the stable code.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,000
This command lists the merge commits on the develop branch.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Each line is one feature or fix branch that was merged: app mvp, tests, docker, jenkins pipeline, ansible, security hardening and more.

5
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,000
This is the real project history.

6
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Let me follow one feature, security hardening.

7
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:40,000
On its branch there are two commits.

8
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:48,000
The first adds the CSRF protection, secure cookies and a database guard against overlapping bookings.

9
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,000
The second adds the tests for them.

10
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:54,000
The prefixes feat and test follow our commit convention.

11
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Small commits, one purpose each.

12
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,000
These are the feature files.

13
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:12,000
The controllers got a CSRF check, the schema got the database triggers, the templates got the token field, and there are new integration tests.

14
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:17,000
Seventeen files, mostly additions, and no unrelated changes.

15
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:21,000
A reviewer can understand the whole feature from this list.

16
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Now the merge itself.

17
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:33,000
This merge commit has two parents: the previous tip of develop, and the last commit of the feature branch.

18
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:42,000
We merged with the no fast forward option, so Git always creates this merge commit, and the history shows the feature as one unit.

19
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,000
If this feature had to be undone, we would revert this single commit.

20
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:58,000
To show the commands themselves safely, I make a small practice repository in the temporary folder, not in the project.

21
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:09,000
I create the develop branch, write a file, run git add, which puts the file in the staging area, then git commit, which saves a snapshot with a message.

22
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,000
This is practice, not project history.

23
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:17,000
Now a feature branch.

24
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:23,000
Git switch with dash c creates feature slash room search and moves me onto it.

25
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:24,000
I edit the file.

26
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:29,000
Git status short shows a space and M: modified but not staged.

27
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:34,000
After git add it shows M in the first column: staged.

28
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Then git commit saves it on the feature branch only.

29
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:39,000
Develop is untouched.

30
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Git push sends my branch to the remote, the shared copy.

31
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:53,000
In this practice the remote is another local folder, and on a real project it would be GitHub.

32
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:00,000
The dash u option links my local branch to the remote one, so later pushes and pulls know where to go.

33
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:04,000
Git branch dash vv shows that link in square brackets.

34
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:11,000
This is the merge, normally done after a pull request is reviewed.

35
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:15,000
I switch to develop and merge the feature with no fast forward.

36
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,000
The graph shows the shape: develop, a side line for the feature, and a merge commit joining them.

37
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:27,000
This is exactly the shape of the real project history we saw earlier.

38
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:35,000
Git pull is the opposite of push: it brings other people's commits to my copy.

39
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:42,000
I push develop, then a second clone, a pretend teammate, makes a commit and pushes it.

40
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:44,000
Now in my repository I run git pull.

41
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:49,000
It fetches the new commit and fast forwards develop to it.

42
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:53,000
Teams run pull often so they work on the latest code.

43
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:00,000
A pull request asks the team to review a branch before it is merged.

44
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:11,000
Our prepared pull request text for security hardening has a summary, how it was tested, and an author checklist, and the reviewer checks a second list.

45
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:12,000
But I must be honest.

46
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,000
This live query to GitHub shows zero pull requests.

47
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:20,000
Our features were reviewed with this checklist and merged locally.

48
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:26,000
Opening real pull requests on GitHub is a remaining step that needs my GitHub login.

49
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:41,000
Last, I delete the three practice folders and confirm that my real project repository is unchanged: same branch, and no changes except this demonstration folder.

50
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:44,000
The practice never touched the project history.


